architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "0005"
  title: Only Block 1 is YAML-compatible
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-07-11"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: |
    Early project documentation claimed SYON is a strict safe subset of
    YAML 1.2, without qualification. That claim doesn't hold for the
    whole language. Block 1 (record syntax: indentation-based mappings
    and sequences using the colon, dash, and hash spacing markers)
    genuinely is a safe subset of YAML 1.2 block style -- every valid
    Block-1-only document is valid YAML. Block 2 (document fences,
    triple-backtick delimited with a path-and-format info string) and
    Block 3 (the literal escape hatch, triple-bracket delimited) are
    SYON-specific syntax with no YAML equivalent. A fence or a
    literal-block delimiter is not valid YAML syntax at all -- a document
    using either block type cannot be parsed by a YAML 1.2 parser, full
    stop.

    This mismatch wasn't caught until a project review of the generated
    docs site pointed out that Block 2 provides multi-document-style
    embedding (SYON deliberately has no YAML-native document-start or
    document-end markers, but Block 2 fences fill that role through
    different, non-YAML syntax) and that Block 3 is not YAML's block
    scalar, despite serving a similar purpose.
  decision: |
    State the YAML relationship precisely: only Block 1 is a strict,
    safe YAML 1.2 subset. SYON as a whole is not YAML-compatible, because
    of Block 2 and Block 3. spec/README.md, spec/02-grammar.md's
    forbidden-set table, the root README.md, and the docs site were
    corrected to this effect.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - The docs no longer oversell YAML compatibility; the actual boundary (Block 1 only) is stated once, in spec/README.md, and other pages link to it instead of restating the claim.
    negative:
      - Anyone reaching for a plain YAML 1.2 parser to consume arbitrary SYON documents will work fine for Block-1-only content, and will fail or silently misinterpret any document using a fence or a literal block. This must not be undersold in documentation again.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: Keep the unqualified "strict safe subset of YAML" claim
      reason: Demonstrably false for any document using Block 2 or Block 3, including the project's own canonical examples.
    -
      name: Redesign Block 2 and Block 3 to be valid YAML syntax
      reason: Tracked separately as optional future work, not this decision -- see issues #2 and #3 for YAML-compatible modes for Block 2 and Block 3 respectively. If either ships, this ADR should be superseded rather than silently reworded.
  amended-by: |
    ADR 0007 removed Block 3, the literal escape hatch, rather than giving it
    a YAML-compatible mode. The reasoning here is unchanged and still holds:
    a construct with no YAML equivalent costs the Block 1 compatibility
    guarantee. Only the inventory changed. The document fence is now the sole
    construct that a YAML 1.2 parser cannot read.
